.TH NETWATCH 1 "2026-11-01" "netwatch" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netwatch \- real-time network diagnostics in your terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netwatch
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B netwatch daemon
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B netwatch doctor
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-check\-capture ]
.RB [ \-\-interface
.IR NAME ]
.br
.B netwatch diagnose
.I SUBCOMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B netwatch uptime
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR PERIOD ]
.RB [ \-\-json " | " \-\-csv ]
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B netwatch geoip update
.RB [ \-\-city ]
.br
.B netwatch resolver
.I SUBCOMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B netwatch
shows live network activity in a terminal UI: per-connection throughput with
application-protocol decoding, process attribution where the platform allows
it, interface and packet views, and a diagnostic engine that learns a baseline
per network and opens an issue when that baseline breaks.
.PP
It runs with no configuration. Packet capture needs platform-specific access. Use
.B netwatch doctor
to inspect setup, then configure restricted capture access (see
.B PERMISSIONS
below).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-remote " URL"
Remote URL for the fleet backend. Requires an API key, from the
.B NETWATCH_API_KEY
environment variable or
.BR \-\-api\-key .
It must be
.B https://
unless
.B \-\-insecure\-remote
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-api\-key " KEY"
Remote API key. Discouraged: any user on the host can read it in
.BR ps (1).
Set the
.B NETWATCH_API_KEY
environment variable instead.
.TP
.B \-\-insecure\-remote
Allow an
.B http://
remote URL. The API key and every snapshot then cross the network in
cleartext, readable by anyone on the path. Without this flag netwatch
refuses such a URL and exits.
.TP
.BI \-\-view " MODE"
Start in
.BR full ,
.B lite
or
.B dense
view.
.TP
.B \-\-lite
Start in Lite view. Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-view lite" .
.TP
.B \-\-demo
Replay the recorded Diagnose demo. Other tabs stay live, and every frame is
marked as a demo.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sandbox
Disable the worker sandbox.
.TP
.B \-\-sandbox\-strict
Require sandbox enforcement; exit rather than run unconfined.
.TP
.B \-\-metrics
Expose daemon metrics on 127.0.0.1:9464.
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-addr " ADDR"
Address for daemon metrics.
.TP
.B \-\-daemon
Headless mode, no terminal UI. Alias:
.BR \-\-headless .
.TP
.B \-\-generate\-config
Write the default configuration file and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B daemon
Run the collectors with no rendering, buffering snapshots for a backend.
.TP
.B doctor
Read-only setup report: what netwatch can and cannot do on this host, with
.B \-\-json
for machine-readable output and
.B \-\-check\-capture
to attempt a real capture.
.TP
.B diagnose run
Diagnose the network for a bounded time, report, and exit. Options:
.B \-\-budget
(a duration from 5s to 10m, default 30s),
.B \-\-target
(a target named in the configuration) and
.B \-\-format
.BR json " or " text ,
and
.B \-\-include\-beta
to let issues from beta rules (whose contract is not yet reviewed) set exit 1.
The exit status is part of the interface: 0, completed with no issue; 1, at
least one open issue; 2, incomplete, no usable observation inside the budget;
3, bad arguments or the session could not start.
.TP
.B diagnose coverage
Which diagnostic checks have their inputs, and why the others do not.
.TP
.B diagnose episodes
List recorded incident episodes.
.TP
.B diagnose replay
Replay a recorded episode through the engine.
.TP
.B diagnose features
Write the feature table of recorded episodes, with
.B \-\-out
and
.BR \-\-schema .
.TP
.B diagnose export
Write a pseudonymised bundle of recent incidents. Nothing is uploaded.
.TP
.B diagnose corpus
For netwatch's developers: regenerate the pinned test corpus under
.IR tests/diagnose/corpus ,
or one entry with
.BR \-\-only .
.TP
.B uptime
Outage history from the uptime log the TUI and
.B daemon
keep: one row per outage with its start, end, duration and the hop that
failed (gateway, internet or DNS), then the outage count, total downtime,
uptime and the time nothing was monitored.
.B \-\-since
takes a period such as 7d (the default), 24h or 90m;
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-csv
give machine-readable output with a versioned schema. A hop is down after
three probe cycles in a row with no reply. Time when netwatch was not running
is reported as not monitored, never as up.
.TP
.B geoip update
Download DB\-IP Lite's Country and ASN databases for offline GeoIP lookups, and
the City database too with
.B \-\-city
or once it has been downloaded. Reports a file as updated or as already current.
An empty
.B geoip_db
or
.B geoip_asn_db
in the configuration uses these files. A running session keeps what it opened
until it is restarted. Nothing refreshes them on its own, and
.B netwatch doctor
flags one over 60 days old. Exits 0 when every file is up to date and 1
otherwise, leaving a file it could not replace as it was. The data source and
the file location may change between releases. IP geolocation by DB\-IP
(https://db\-ip.com), CC BY 4.0.
.TP
.B resolver status
Report the current DNS resolver and whether netwatch could change it.
.TP
.B resolver set
Temporarily point the resolver at an address. Only for administrator-confirmed
unmanaged files, and requires
.BR \-\-unmanaged .
.TP
.B resolver recover
Put a resolver changed by netwatch back.
.SH KEYS
.B 1
to
.B 9
and
.B 0
switch tabs,
.B V
cycles the three views,
.B L
opens Lite view,
.B ?
lists every key, and
.B q
quits.
.SH PERMISSIONS
Live capture needs elevated access. On Linux, rather than using sudo, grant the
capabilities to a root-owned binary that only members of a
.B netwatch
group can run, then log in again for the membership to take effect:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo groupadd \-f netwatch
sudo usermod \-aG netwatch "$USER"
bin=$(echo "$PATH" | tr : '\en' | grep \-vx '/usr/local/bin/*' | while read \-r dir; do
  [ \-x "$dir/netwatch" ] && echo "$dir/netwatch" && break
done)
bin=$(readlink \-f "${bin:\-/usr/local/bin/netwatch}")
case $bin in
  /usr/bin/netwatch | /usr/local/bin/netwatch) ;;
  *)
    sudo install \-o root \-g netwatch \-m 0750 "$bin" /usr/local/bin/netwatch
    bin=/usr/local/bin/netwatch
    ;;
esac
sudo chown root:netwatch "$bin"
sudo chmod 0750 "$bin"
sudo setcap 'cap_net_raw,cap_bpf,cap_perfmon+eip' "$bin"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The recipe grants the first netwatch on PATH outside
.IR /usr/local/bin ,
with symlinks followed. A package's
.I /usr/bin/netwatch
is changed in place. Any other binary, such as one from cargo install,
Homebrew or a Nix profile, is copied to
.I /usr/local/bin
first; run that copy. Because the search skips
.IR /usr/local/bin ,
a re-run after an upgrade copies the new binary over the old copy.
Capabilities attach to a specific file, so they must be re-applied after every
install or upgrade. Never grant them to a binary every
account can run. Running as root works, but containment is then limited. On
Windows, install Npcap first.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/netwatch/config.toml
Configuration. Create it with
.BR "netwatch \-\-generate\-config" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/netwatch/egress\-policy.toml
Declared egress policy. Alerts fire for destinations blocked here; set
.B alert = "all"
to alert on everything outside the allowlist as well.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/netwatch/exports/
Packet captures (PCAPNG and PCAP), incident bundles and Diagnose reports.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/netwatch/geoip/
Databases
.B netwatch geoip update
downloaded.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/netwatch/uptime/
The uptime log
.B netwatch uptime
reads, one file per day, kept 90 days. Turn it off with
.BR "record_uptime = false" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
on a usage error or a failed command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tcpdump (1),
.BR ss (8),
.BR setcap (8)
.PP
Full documentation: https://github.com/matthart1983/netwatch
